<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Food I Eat During a Celebration – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Festival Season.</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Food I Eat During a Celebration</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a word or phrase to a group story or project with support or guidance. Interactive: Write simple messages to peers using words, drawings, and short phrases.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a word or phrase to respond to a poll or questions about festivals (e.g., "Let's have a poll. Do you like festivals? Write yes or no."). Can write short messages about festival foods and activities collaboratively (e.g., "I eat cotton candy at the festival."). Can add a word or phrase to a group story with guidance (e.g., "The ______ are loud." "What is loud?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of festival foods (cotton candy, corn on the cob, shaved ice). Students say the names of the foods together. Teacher points to a flashcard and asks, "What do you like to eat at a festival?" Students respond with "Cotton candy!" or "Corn!"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of "cotton candy." The teacher says: "Cotton candy. I like cotton candy. It is colorful." The teacher writes "Cotton candy is colorful" on the board, demonstrating clear, simple writing. The teacher highlights the /k/ sound in "cotton" and /f/ in "colorful" when writing. The teacher then writes "I like cotton candy" while saying it.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I write about?" (Expected: Cotton candy!)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'colorful' an adjective?" (Expected: Yes!)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher displays a simple "Festival Food Poster" with pictures of various festival foods (cotton candy, corn on the cob, shaved ice, fireworks, music) and short, descriptive sentences next to them. For example:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture of cotton candy: "I eat cotton candy. It is colorful."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture of corn on the cob: "I eat corn on the cob. It is big."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture of fireworks: "I watch fireworks. They are bright." (Highlight /f/ in "fireworks")</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short descriptive sentences about festival foods and activities presented on a large "Festival Food Poster" with clear illustrations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can identify words and simple phrases related to festivals and food when presented visually.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What food is colorful?" Students point to "cotton candy."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What do I eat that is big?" Students point to "corn on the cob."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True or False: "The music is quiet." (Teacher points to "music" picture). Students say "False! Music is loud!" (emphasize "loud").</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing short sentences using "I eat/watch..." + noun + "It/They is/are..." + adjective. They will use vocabulary like 'cotton candy', 'corn on the cob', 'shaved ice', 'fireworks', 'music', 'dance', and adjectives like 'colorful', 'big', 'bright', 'loud', 'fun'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Fill in the Blanks:</b> The teacher gives students a worksheet with simple sentences about festival foods, but with missing words. Students look at pictures and fill in the blanks.
                    <br>Example: "I eat __________. It is __________. (Picture of shaved ice, fun) -> "I eat <b>shaved ice</b>. It is <b>fun</b>."
                    <br>Example: "I watch __________. They are __________. (Picture of bright fireworks) -> "I watch <b>fireworks</b>. They are <b>bright</b>." (Teacher explicitly models the writing of "fireworks" and the /f/ sound.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Starters:</b> The teacher provides sentence starters and a word bank. Students choose words to complete sentences, focusing on correct subject-verb agreement and adjectives.
                    <br>Word Bank: <i>cotton candy, corn on the cob, loud, colorful, big, fun</i>
                    <br>Sentence Starters:
                    <br>a. I eat __________. It is __________.
                    <br>b. The music is __________. It is __________. (Focus on "music" and /m/ sound)
                    <br>c. I like the __________. It is __________.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. They read their completed sentences from the worksheet to their partner. Partners check if the words chosen make sense and if they are written clearly. They offer positive feedback: "Good job!" or "I like your writing!"</li>
            </ol>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Story Writing. Students will contribute a word or short phrase to a large class "Festival Food Story" poster. Each student will draw their chosen food and write one simple sentence or phrase about it.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces a large poster titled "Our Festival Food Story."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher starts the story: "At the festival, we eat..."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a small piece of paper. They draw their favorite festival food (from the practiced vocabulary: cotton candy, corn on the cob, shaved ice, etc.).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below their drawing, students write one simple sentence or phrase describing it or their preference, with teacher support. Examples: "I like shaved ice. It is cold." "Cotton candy is colorful." "The corn is big."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns coming to the front, showing their drawing, reading their sentence aloud (if comfortable), and then gluing their contribution to the class "Festival Food Story" poster. The teacher helps with spelling and sentence structure as needed.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the entire class story aloud at the end.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">12 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the production task. The teacher circulates, observing students' ability to write a word or phrase, their use of target vocabulary, and their effort in drawing and participating in the group story.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student's individual contribution (drawing and written sentence/phrase) to the "Our Festival Food Story" poster.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Student attempts to use at least one festival food noun (e.g., "cotton candy," "corn") or adjective (e.g., "colorful," "big," "fun").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Message:</b> The written word/phrase is legible and generally understandable.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Participation:</b> Student contributes their drawing and writing to the group poster.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer two quick questions by circling a smiley face (happy, neutral, sad) or verbally confirming.</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I write about festival food today?" (Yes / No)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it fun to write our story?" (Yes / No)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new festival food word did you write today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or hard about writing?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., The collaborative poster motivated students, the sentence frames provided good scaffolding.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or similar writing tasks? (e.g., Some students needed more explicit modeling for sentence construction, maybe provide pre-written words to trace for those struggling with fine motor skills.)</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize the "Our Festival Food Story" to another class or present parts of it, integrating the writing they produced today with speaking and listening skills, and potentially creating a small booklet or digital presentation of their story.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>